<section>
<title>Problems</title>
<exercise xml:id="prob_ThreeCompress">
<statement>
<p>
Three identical gas-cylinder systems are compressed from the
same initial state to final states that have the same volume, one
isothermally, one adiabatically, and one isobarically. Which
system has the most work done on it? The least?
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_monatomic_vs_diatomic">
<statement>
<p>
The relation between thermal energy and temperature depends on the
type of gas molecule: for a monatomic gas, <m>\Delta E_\text{therm}  =
\frac{3}{2}nR\Delta T</m> and for a diatomic gas, <m>\Delta E_\text{therm}  =
\frac{5}{2}nR\Delta T</m>. In your own words <mdash/> and discussing explicitly
the microscopic picture of gases <mdash/> explain why it is that you need to
put more thermal energy into a diatomic gas than for a monatomic gas to
raise the temperature by <m>1\,\text{K}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_pistonA">
<statement>
<p>
Consider a piston in a car engine that is compressing an air-gasoline
mixture before ignition, all in the gas state.
<ol marker="a)">
<li><p>Under what circumstances would the compression be adiabatic? 
(There are a couple of ways to get an adiabatic compression, one of
which is more relevant to a car engine.)</p></li>
<li><p>Use the First Law of Thermodynamics to argue that the gas
  temperature increases if the compression is adiabatic.  Where does
  that additional thermal energy come from during this process?</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
A gas with <m>n</m> moles of molecules starts with a temperature <m>T_1</m>,
pressure <m>p_1</m> and volume <m>V_1</m>. Draw a <m>p</m><nbsp/>-<m>V</m> diagram for the following
multi-step processes (and label each process with its letter a, b, or c):
<ol marker="a)">
<li><p>The gas expands at a constant pressure to twice its initial
volume.</p></li>
<li><p>The gas is then compressed isothermally back to its initial
volume.</p></li>
<li><p>The gas then expands adiabatically back to its initial
pressure.</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
A gas with <m>n</m> moles of molecules starts with a temperature
<m>T_1</m>, pressure <m>p_1</m> and volume <m>V_1</m>. Draw a <m>p</m><nbsp/>-<m>V</m> diagram
for the following multi-step processes:
<ol marker="a)">
<li><p>The gas is heated up at constant volume, doubling its
initial temperature.</p></li>
<li><p>The gas expands adiabatically until the pressure returns
to <m>1.5p_1</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>The container no longer expands but a vent is opened,
  allowing gas to escape until the pressure returns to the original
  pressure <m>p_1</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>The gas is compressed isothermally until the volume returns to the
original volume <m>V_1</m>.</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_PVProblem">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the work done on a gas as it undergoes the process shown
in the <m>p</m><nbsp/>-<m>V</m> diagram (<xref ref="fig_PVProblem">Figure</xref>).
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig_PVProblem" >
<caption>For Prob. <xref ref="prob_PVProblem"></xref>.</caption>
<image width="46%" source="PVProblem" />
</figure>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_pistonB">
<statement>
<p>
A piston initially contains <m>1.2\,\text{mol}</m> of a monatomic gas at a pressure
of <m>210\,\text{kPa}</m>, and a volume of <m>0.25\,\text{L}</m>. The gas expands
at constant pressure to a volume of <m>0.47\,\text{L}</m>. Calculate the
work done on the gas during this process.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_pistonC">
<statement>
<p>
A piston initially contains <m>0.35\,\text{mol}</m> of a diatomic gas at a pressure
of <m>105\,\text{kPa}</m>, a temperature <m>350\,\text{K}</m> and a volume of
<m>9.7\,\text{L}</m>. The gas is ignited, raising the temperature rapidly to
<m>550\,\text{K}</m> without a significant change in the volume.
Calculate the work done on the gas during this process.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_isotherm_process">
<statement>
<p>
A monatomic ideal gas undergoes an isothermal compression from an initial
volume of <m>7.0\,\text{L}</m> and pressure <m>100\,\text{kPa}</m> to a final volume
and pressure of <m>2.0\,\text{L}</m> and <m>350\,\text{kPa}</m>, respectively.
The temperature along the isotherm is <m>120^\circ \,\text{C}</m>.
<ol marker="a)">
<li><p>Determine the number of moles of this gas.</p></li>
<li><p>Determine the work done <em>on the gas</em> during this process.</p></li>
<li><p>Determine the heat flowing into the gas during this process.</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_adiabatic">
<statement>
<p>
Consider a fixed amount of an ideal monatomic gas undergoing the
<em>adiabatic</em> process illustrated in <xref ref="fig_gasProcess2">Figure</xref>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig_gasProcess2" >
<caption>For <xref ref="prob_adiabatic">problem</xref>.</caption>
<image width="39%" source="gasProcess2" />
</figure>
<ol marker="a)">
<li><p>Calculate the number of moles in the gas.</p></li>
<li><p>Calculate the work done on the gas between points <em>A</em>
and <em>B</em>.</p></li>
</ol>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_flattium">
<statement>
<ol marker="a)">
<li><p>Use the Equipartition Theorem to determine a relation between the
thermal energy change <m>\Delta E_\text{therm}</m> and the temperature
change <m>\Delta T</m> for a monatomic <em>flattium</em> gas where the
molecules can move only in two dimensions.</p></li>
<li><p>Do the same thing, but this time for a diatomic flattium gas.</p></li>
</ol>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
In <xref ref="prob_monatomic_vs_diatomic">problem</xref> you explained
the different factors, <m>\frac{5}{2}</m> vs.<nbsp/><m>\frac{3}{2}</m>,
that appear in thermal energy expressions for diatomic and monatomic gases.
The expressions differ because of the additional rotational degrees of
freedom for the diatomic molecules, which adds terms to the energy
used in the equipartition theorem. But if the gas is
<em>really</em> hot, it is also possible to excite <em>vibrations</em> in the
diatomic molecules, resulting in two more terms in the equation for the energy
of a diatomic molecule: one for the kinetic energy of the vibration and
one for the potential energy of the vibrating molecule. What would you
expect for the relation between <m>\Delta E_\text{therm}</m> and <m>\Delta T</m>
for such a hot diatomic gas?
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_pistonD">
<statement>
<p>
A piston contains 0.65 moles of diatomic nitrogen gas (N<m>_2</m>),
with an initial pressure and volume of <m>110\,\text{kPa}</m> and
<m>0.15\,\text{L}</m>, respectively. The piston compresses the gas
adiabatically to a volume of <m>0.062\,\text{L}</m>.
Calculate the pressure of the gas after this compression.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
A piston contains <m>0.45\,\text{mol}</m> of monatomic helium gas, with an initial
volume and temperature of <m>0.12\,\text{L}</m> and <m>285\,\text{K}</m>, respectively. The
piston expands the gas adiabatically to a volume of <m>0.31\,\text{L}</m>.
Calculate the temperature of the gas after this expansion.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_chart">
<statement>
<p>
A fixed amount of diatomic ideal gas goes through the cycle as shown
in <xref ref="fig_pv_diagram">Figure</xref>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig_pv_diagram" >
<caption>For <xref ref="prob_chart">problem</xref>.</caption>
<image width="50%" source="pv_diagram.png" />
</figure>
<p>
<m>\textbf{A}\to \textbf{B}</m>: adiabatic compression
</p>

<p>
<m>\textbf{B}\to \textbf{C}</m>: constant-pressure expansion
</p>

<p>
<m>\textbf{C}\to \textbf{A}</m>: cooling at constant volume
</p>

<p>
Fill in the chart below for the change in thermal energy, heat flow
in, and work done <em>on the gas</em> for each of the processes and for the
complete cycle. <em>Show all work.</em>
<me>
  <image width="75%" source="chart.png" />
</me>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig_pv_processes" >
<caption>For <xref ref="prob_table">problem</xref>.</caption>
<image width="46%" source="pv_processes.png" />
</figure>
<exercise xml:id="prob_table">
<statement>
<p>
<m>0.20\,\text{mol}</m> of diatomic ideal gas undergoes the
processes shown in the <m>p</m><nbsp/>-<m>V</m> diagram of <xref ref="fig_pv_processes">Figure</xref>.
The table below has boxes for the change in thermal energy of the
gas, the heat added to the gas, and the work done <em>on</em> the gas,
all in joules. Energy values have already been entered in two of the
boxes. Fill in the remaining entries with values accurate to three
significant digits. Be sure to show your work for each entry.
</p>

<p>
<image width="84%" source="table" />
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_from2011final">
<statement>
<p>
The <m>p</m><nbsp/>-<m>V</m> diagram in <xref ref="fig_from2011final">Figure</xref> shows a cyclic
process for 2 moles of an ideal monatomic gas.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig_from2011final" >
<caption>For <xref ref="prob_from2011final">problem</xref>.</caption>
<image width="46%" source="pVfinal" />
</figure>
<ol marker="a)">
<li><p>Determine the temperature of the gas at state <em>A</em>.</p></li>
<li><p>Determine the heat flow into the gas during the process 
<em>A</em><m>\rightarrow</m><em>B</em>.</p></li>
<li><p>Determine the heat flow into the gas during the process 
<em>B</em><m>\rightarrow</m><em>C</em>.</p></li>
<li><p>Is the work done on the system for the complete cycle
positive, negative or zero? (No calculation required.)</p></li>
</ol>
</statement>
</exercise>
</section>
